<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript</AssemblyName>
    <Description>The Broiler.VM JavaScript language profile: descriptor, verifier, executor, value and object model, standard library, host imports and payload projections. It implements the pinned ECMA-262 edition under the manifest broiler.javascript.wide, with the optional surfaces a composition admits beside it; the profile's support table, drafted and not issued, and its status ledger are the authority for what exists.</Description>

    <!-- JS-0 declares no package; see the Format project for why. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>

    <!-- JsNativePage is the one place in this product that maps memory and makes it executable,
         and its mapping calls are in that file and its two platform halves. The property is also
         needed by the code that enters mapped code and is entered from it - the numeric arm and its
         probe (JsNativeExecution, JsNativeAbi) and the baseline form's entry, activation and handler
         table (JsEngine.Baseline, JsNativeActivation, JsBaselineHandlers) - none of which maps
         anything. The property is here rather than nowhere so that the capability is a visible,
         reviewable line in a project file instead of an assumption; a reader who wants to know
         whether this assembly can touch raw memory reads one line to find out. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ADR 0011 obligation P1: a profile's Broiler.VM-owned reference set is exactly Abstractions
    and Binary, and P1's 2026-08-31 revision states that a profile component's own siblings are
    not members of that set. The format assembly is such a sibling.

    This project never references the lowering. That absence is what makes an execution-only
    composition contain a format, a verifier and an interpreter and no compiler at all, and
    rule N2 asserts it rather than leaving it to review.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Broiler.VM.Abstractions\Broiler.VM.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Broiler.VM.Binary\Broiler.VM.Binary.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Format\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Format.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE SHARED ALGORITHMS LIBRARY (ADR 0014; JSD-0065, milestone SJL-3.4): the runtime's number conversions: Number::toString at every radix, toFixed, toExponential and toPrecision, StringToNumber, parseInt and parseFloat, the string white-space set, and the array-index and canonical numeric key tests. The
    package comes from the committed feed eng/packages/ at its pin, the lock file records its
    content hash, and rule I1 holds both to the allow-list, which names this project.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <!-- A restore for one runtime identifier - a trimmed or Native AOT publish - adds that RID's
         own packages (the IL compiler, the linker tasks) to the graph. It writes its lock file under
         obj/, so the committed lock file stays the RID-less one CI restores in locked mode and rule
         I1 reads, and a publish leaves the tree as it found it. -->
    <NuGetLockFilePath Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/obj/packages.$(RuntimeIdentifier).lock.json</NuGetLockFilePath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
